"posix_fadvise(2) - Linux manual page (https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/posix_fadvise.2.html)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/posix_fadvise.2.html\",\"lineno\":75}); Total lines: 209\nL0: 【0†man7.org】 > Linux > 【1†man-pages】  | 【2†Linux/UNIX system programming training】\nL1: \nL2: * * *\nL3: \nL4: # posix_fadvise(2) — Linux manual page\nL5: \nL6: 【3†NAME】 | 【4†LIBRARY】 | 【5†SYNOPSIS】 | 【6†DESCRIPTION】 | 【7†RETURN VALUE】 | 【8†ERRORS】 | 【9†VERSIONS】 | 【10†STANDARDS】 | 【11†HISTORY】 | 【12†NOTES】 | 【13†BUGS】 | 【14†SEE ALSO】 | 【15†COLOPHON】\nL7: [Input] [Input] [Input]  |\nL8: \nL9:     posix_fadvise(2)           System Calls Manual           posix_fadvise(2)\nL10: ## NAME         【16†top】\nL11: \nL12:            posix_fadvise - predeclare an access pattern for file data\nL13: \nL14: \nL15: ## LIBRARY         【16†top】\nL16: \nL17:            Standard C library (libc, -lc)\nL18: \nL19: \nL20: ## SYNOPSIS         【16†top】\nL21: \nL22:            #include <fcntl.h>\nL23: \nL24:            int posix_fadvise(int fd, off_t offset, off_t size, int advice);\nL25: \nL26:        Feature Test Macro Requirements for glibc (see\nL27:        【17†feature_test_macros(7)】):\nL28: \nL29:            posix_fadvise():\nL30:                _POSIX_C_SOURCE >= 200112L\nL31: ## DESCRIPTION         【16†top】\nL32: \nL33:            Programs can use posix_fadvise() to announce an intention to\nL34:            access file data in a specific pattern in the future, thus\nL35:            allowing the kernel to perform appropriate optimizations.\nL36: \nL37:            The advice applies to a (not necessarily existent) region starting\nL38:            at offset and extending for size bytes (or until the end of the\nL39:            file if size is 0) within the file referred to by fd.  The advice\nL40:            is not binding; it merely constitutes an expectation on behalf of\nL41:            the application.\nL42: \nL43:            Permissible values for advice include:\nL44: \nL45:            POSIX_FADV_NORMAL\nL46:                   Indicates that the application has no advice to give about\nL47:                   its access pattern for the specified data.  If no advice is\nL48:                   given for an open file, this is the default assumption.\nL49: \nL50:            POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL\nL51:                   The application expects to access the specified data\nL52:                   sequentially (with lower offsets read before higher ones).\nL53: \nL54:            POSIX_FADV_RANDOM\nL55:                   The specified data will be accessed in random order.\nL56: \nL57:            POSIX_FADV_NOREUSE\nL58:                   The specified data will be accessed only once.\nL59: \nL60:                   Before Linux 2.6.18, POSIX_FADV_NOREUSE had the same\nL61:                   semantics as POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED.  This was probably a bug;\nL62:                   from Linux 2.6.18 until Linux 6.2 this flag was a no-op.\nL63:                   Since Linux 6.3, POSIX_FADV_NOREUSE signals that the kernel\nL64:                   page replacement algorithm can ignore access to mapped page\nL65:                   cache marked by this flag.  This is useful, for example,\nL66:                   while streaming large files.\nL67: \nL68:            POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED\nL69:                   The specified data will be accessed in the near future.\nL70:                   POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED initiates a nonblocking read of the\nL71:                   specified region into the page cache.  The amount of data\nL72:                   read may be decreased by the kernel depending on virtual\nL73:                   memory load.  (A few megabytes will usually be fully\nL74:                   satisfied, and more is rarely useful.)\nL75: \nL76:            POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED\nL77:                   The specified data will not be accessed in the near future.\nL78:                   POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED attempts to free cached pages\nL79:                   associated with the specified region.  This is useful, for\nL80:                   example, while streaming large files.  A program may\nL81:                   periodically request the kernel to free cached data that\nL82:                   has already been used, so that more useful cached pages are\nL83:                   not discarded instead.\nL84: \nL85:                   Requests to discard partial pages are ignored.  It is\nL86:                   preferable to preserve needed data than discard unneeded\nL87:                   data.  If the application requires that data be considered\nL88:                   for discarding, then offset and size must be page-aligned.\nL89: \nL90:                   The implementation may attempt to write back dirty pages in\nL91:                   the specified region, but this is not guaranteed.  Any\nL92:                   unwritten dirty pages will not be freed.  If the\nL93:                   application wishes to ensure that dirty pages will be\nL94:                   released, it should call 【18†fsync(2)】 or 【19†fdatasync(2)】 first.\nL95: ## RETURN VALUE         【16†top】\nL96: \nL97:            On success, zero is returned.  On error, an error number is\nL98:            returned.\nL99: ## ERRORS         【16†top】\nL100: \nL101:            EBADF  The fd argument was not a valid file descriptor.\nL102: \nL103:            EINVAL An invalid value was specified for advice.\nL104: \nL105:            ESPIPE The specified file descriptor refers to a pipe or FIFO.\nL106:                   (ESPIPE is the error specified by POSIX, but before Linux\nL107:                   2.6.16, Linux returned EINVAL in this case.)\nL108: ## VERSIONS         【16†top】\nL109: \nL110:            Under Linux, POSIX_FADV_NORMAL sets the readahead window to the\nL111:            default size for the backing device; POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL doubles\nL112:            this size, and POSIX_FADV_RANDOM disables file readahead entirely.\nL113:            POSIX_FADV_NOREUSE does not modify the readahead window size.\nL114:            These changes affect the entire file, not just the specified\nL115:            region (but other open file handles to the same file are\nL116:            unaffected).\nL117:        C library/kernel differences\nL118:            The name of the wrapper function in the C library is\nL119:            posix_fadvise().  The underlying system call is called fadvise64()\nL120:            (or, on some architectures, fadvise64_64()); the difference\nL121:            between the two is that the former system call assumes that the\nL122:            type of the size argument is size_t, while the latter expects\nL123:            loff_t there.\nL124: \nL125:        Architecture-specific variants\nL126:            Some architectures require 64-bit arguments to be aligned in a\nL127:            suitable pair of registers (see 【20†syscall(2)】 for further detail).\nL128:            On such architectures, the call signature of posix_fadvise() shown\nL129:            in the SYNOPSIS would force a register to be wasted as padding\nL130:            between the fd and offset arguments.  Therefore, these\nL131:            architectures define a version of the system call that orders the\nL132:            arguments suitably, but is otherwise exactly the same as\nL133:            posix_fadvise().\nL134: \nL135:            For example, since Linux 2.6.14, ARM has the following system\nL136:            call:\nL137: \nL138:                long arm_fadvise64_64(int fd, int advice,\nL139:                                      loff_t offset, loff_t size);\nL140: \nL141:            These architecture-specific details are generally hidden from\nL142:            applications by the glibc posix_fadvise() wrapper function, which\nL143:            invokes the appropriate architecture-specific system call.\nL144: ## STANDARDS         【16†top】\nL145: \nL146:            POSIX.1-2024.\nL147: ## HISTORY         【16†top】\nL148: \nL149:            POSIX.1-2001.\nL150: \nL151:            Kernel support first appeared in Linux 2.5.60; the underlying\nL152:            system call is called fadvise64().  Library support has been\nL153:            provided since glibc 2.2, via the wrapper function\nL154:            posix_fadvise().\nL155: \nL156:            Since Linux 3.18, support for the underlying system call is\nL157:            optional, depending on the setting of the CONFIG_ADVISE_SYSCALLS\nL158:            configuration option.\nL159:            The type of the size argument was changed from size_t to off_t in\nL160:            POSIX.1-2001 TC1.\nL161: ## NOTES         【16†top】\nL162: \nL163:            The contents of the kernel buffer cache can be cleared via the\nL164:            /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches interface described in 【21†proc(5)】.\nL165: \nL166:            One can obtain a snapshot of which pages of a file are resident in\nL167:            the buffer cache by opening a file, mapping it with 【22†mmap(2)】, and\nL168:            then applying 【23†mincore(2)】 to the mapping.\nL169: ## BUGS         【16†top】\nL170: \nL171:            Before Linux 2.6.6, if size was specified as 0, then this was\nL172:            interpreted literally as \"zero bytes\", rather than as meaning \"all\nL173:            bytes through to the end of the file\".\nL174: \nL175: \nL176: ## SEE ALSO         【16†top】\nL177: \nL178:            【24†fincore(1)】, 【23†mincore(2)】, 【25†readahead(2)】, 【26†sync_file_range(2)】,\nL179:            【27†posix_fallocate(3)】, 【28†posix_madvise(3)】\nL180: ## COLOPHON         【16†top】\nL181: \nL182:            This page is part of the man-pages (Linux kernel and C library\nL183:            user-space interface documentation) project.  Information about\nL184:            the project can be found at\nL185:            ⟨【29†https://www.kernel.org/doc/man-pages/†www.kernel.org】⟩.  If you have a bug report\nL186:            for this manual page, see\nL187:            ⟨【30†https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/docs/man-pages/man-pages.git/tree/CONTRIBUTING†git.kernel.org】⟩.\nL188:            This page was obtained from the tarball man-pages-6.19.tar.gz\nL189:            fetched from\nL190:            ⟨【31†https://mirrors.edge.kernel.org/pub/linux/docs/man-pages/†mirrors.edge.kernel.org】⟩ on\nL191:            2026-09-09.  If you discover any rendering problems in this HTML\nL192:            version of the page, or you believe there is a better or more up-\nL193:            to-date source for the page, or you have corrections or\nL194:            improvements to the information in this COLOPHON (which is not\nL195:            part of the original manual page), send a mail to\nL196:            man-pages@man7.org\nL197: \nL198:     Linux man-pages 6.19            2026-02-08               posix_fadvise(2)\nL199: * * *\nL200: \nL201: Pages that refer to this page: 【32†fadvise(1)】,  【33†strace(1)】,  【18†fsync(2)】,  【34†io_uring_enter2(2)】,  【35†io_uring_enter(2)】,  【23†mincore(2)】,  【25†readahead(2)】,  【20†syscall(2)】,  【36†syscalls(2)】,  【37†io_uring_prep_fadvise(3)】,  【38†io_uring_prep_fadvise64(3)】,  【39†off_t(3type)】,  【27†posix_fallocate(3)】,  【28†posix_madvise(3)】\nL202: \nL203: * * *\nL204: \nL205: * * *\nL206: HTML rendering created 2026-09-09 by 【40†Michael Kerrisk】, author of 【41†The Linux Programming Interface】. For details of in-depth Linux/UNIX system programming training courses that I teach, look 【42†here】. Hosting by 【43†jambit GmbH†www.jambit.com】.  |  |\nL207: \nL208: * * *\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nstatesync-gkr/LICENSE at main · Oraclizer/statesync-gkr · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/LICENSE)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"id\":102}); Total lines: 565\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nstatesync-gkr/docs/architecture.md at main · Oraclizer/statesync-gkr · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/docs/architecture.md)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"id\":121}); Total lines: 233\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Oraclizer/statesync-gkr: Rust prover for sparse-Merkle state transitions, with GKR and sumcheck mechanized in Isabelle/HOL · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"zero knowledge\"}); Total lines: 523\nL376: The field, Poseidon2 and challenger primitives come from pinned Plonky3 crates. Rayon schedules CPU workers. The SMT compiler, GKR assembly, sumcheck implementation, sparse wiring, preparation reuse and batch orchestration are implemented in this repository.\nL377: The shipped GKR API uses KoalaBear circuit values and degree-four extension challenges with Poseidon2. Other applications need their own frontend and must check the residual input claims returned by the protocol verifier. There is no bundled compiler for arbitrary Rust programs or a runtime field/hash selection API.\nL378: ## Architecture\nL379: \nL380: 【146†Image: Host code enters the facade, which composes the sparse-Merkle compiler, the verification owner, batching, and the external wrap crate over shared primitives】\nL381: \nL382: Eight crates own one concern each, and the dependency direction is a frozen design boundary:\nL383: \nL384:     `ssgkr-primitives -> ssgkr-sumcheck -> ssgkr-protocol -> ssgkr-compiler\nL385:          -> {ssgkr-batching, ssgkr-commitment}\nL386:               -> {ssgkr-verification, ssgkr-wrap}\nL387:                    -> statesync-gkr facade\nL388:     `\nL389: The generic protocol crates never learn sparse-Merkle specifics, so `ssgkr-sumcheck` and `ssgkr-protocol` are reusable on their own. `ssgkr-primitives` is the only crate allowed to name a `p3-*` path, which confines the pinned plonky3 dependency to one adapter.\nL390: The state-to-proof path runs from native operation semantics, through circuit compilation and witness layout, through per-layer sumcheck, to a verifier check that reduces to the input-layer claim. The transcript binds a domain tag, circuit digest, public inputs, layer claims, and round messages in a fixed order, so proof bytes cannot depend on worker count or scheduling.\nL391: Crate ownership, trust boundaries, the failure model, and the frozen compatibility interfaces are described in 【91†ARCHITECTURE.md】. The separation between the internal proof and the external proof role is recorded in 【147†ADR-0002】. 【148†Project layout†oraclizer.io】 shows how each crate is imported through the `statesync-gkr` facade.\nL392: ## Model verification and implementation boundary\nL393: \nL394: Compiler-model correctness assumes the stated hash-stack behavior; the protocol's challenge assumptions are explicit.\nL395: \nL396: The Isabelle development connects SMT operation semantics, compiler-model acceptance and GKR assembly soundness, and mechanizes batching preservation and the exact degree-four KoalaBear extension. Registered concrete instances establish that the model assumptions can be satisfied.\nL397: The verifier acceptance work proves the forward implication from related acceptance traces to the GKR reduction-chain event. The former local-bridge premise is no longer assumed: it is derived from the concrete round checks and the finish-evaluation part of the Rust/model value relation. Recorded Creusot proofs establish selected public-input and acceptance-classifier contracts.\nL398: Narrowing whole-verifier translation opt-outs to foreign-field equality seams exposes the surrounding round, rejection, carry and final-input logic to contract translation, while production wiring-oracle evaluations, transcript operations and the circuit and public-input observation helpers remain trusted translation boundaries. These artifacts let developers trace and re-check the acceptance rules and reuse that review basis for integration and maintenance.\nL399: The theorem's Rust/model value relation is an explicit premise, and 【100†FORMAL_VERIFICATION.md】 states the exact results, assumptions and scope. Zero knowledge is provided by the external proof wrapper layer.\nL400: \nL401: 【149†Image: Compiler correctness and reduction soundness join and compose into one bound on the full state-to-proof path, discharged on a concrete non-vacuous instance, while batching holds pointwise over the whole path】\nL402: ## External execution evidence\nL403: \nL404: The frozen proof identity has also been exercised outside a single developer machine, and the records ship in this tree:\nL405:   * The published proof binds an exact RISC Zero program identity. 【150†`spikes/zkvm-wrap/identity/`】 fixes the expected program record, and a public policy check rejects any undeclared change to it.\nL406:   * A secure succinct proof of the current v1.2 program identity was accepted by the zkVerify RISC0 pallet on the public Volta test chain and aggregated into an authenticated receipt. The exact statement, aggregation coordinates, and resulting receipt tuple are recorded in 【151†`release/v1.2/PUBLIC_INPUTS.json`】 and 【152†`release/v1.2/PROOF_MANIFEST.json`】.\nL407: The v1.1 program's submission stays recorded in 【153†`release/v1.1/PROOF_MANIFEST.json`】, the v1.0 program's submission stays frozen in 【154†`tests/vectors/risc0-route-b-volta-2.0.0-runtime-rebind-v1.json`】 with its transcript, and the codec is specified in 【155†`docs/encoding/risc0-route-b-manifest-v1.md`】.\nL408:   * Source reproduction is a first-class interface: 【156†`release/v1.2/SOURCE_RECIPE.md`】 separates anonymous source reproduction, which any machine can run against the pinned toolchain and the documented deterministic proof digest, from recorded exact-identity reproduction, and 【157†`release/v1.2/verify.py`】 replays the recorded hashes.\nL409: ## Proof-bound source\nL410: The current source has its own recorded program identity: two controlled clean builds of the identity source selection produced the same eight identity outputs, with image ID `1a8b55f4e1a5f703386dcfc07e5e8b2e42b5adade6be3d0057f2d565f75eb131`. The expected record is 【158†`risc0-route-b-guest-expected-v1.2.json`】, and 【159†IDENTITY_DISTRIBUTION.json】 states the source, identity and proof boundaries.\nL411: The attached external proof, `statesync-gkr-v1.2-proof.cbor`, is bound to this image ID; its size, digest, production run and public verifier receipt are recorded in 【152†`release/v1.2/PROOF_MANIFEST.json`】. The v1.1 proof remains a record of the v1.1 program identity, image ID `dc9a5f0da608178bbe56e98604cb895060c555c18329846b453f71d562d16530`, in 【153†`release/v1.1/PROOF_MANIFEST.json`】.\nL412: All 79 protected files are checked byte for byte, so comments, diagnostics, package descriptions, canonical vectors and field order stay checked. A different build result is a different identity and is never adopted by editing an expected hash. The frozen surface also retains a finite list of historical source annotations, which are not current workflows, approvals, runtime roles, or endorsements; every one of them is defined in 【160†docs/frozen-source-identifiers.md】.\nL413: ## Repository map\nL414:   * `crates/` - primitives, sumcheck, protocol, compiler, batching, commitment, verification, and external-wrap owners;\nL415:   * `src/` - public facade and developer binaries;\nL416:   * `formal/isabelle/` - canonical Isabelle theory sources;\nL417:   * `verif/` - Creusot and Why3 replay records;\nL418:   * `tests/` - public vectors and positive and negative controls;\nL419:   * `examples/` - runnable prove-and-verify and reference-transition paths;\nL420:   * `spikes/zkvm-wrap/` - selected zkVM integration source and exact identity recipe, retained at its identity-bound path;\nL421:   * `benches/` - development measurement reports, with the harness in `src/bin/measure.rs`, and `benches/serving/`, a separate Cargo workspace with the serving components, reference proof server and load frontend;\nL422:   * `docs/` - architecture decisions, encodings, generated proof documents, and frozen identifier definitions;\nL423:   * `release/v1.2/` - public surface verifier, source recipe, manifests, and release evidence;\nL424:   * `release/v1.1/` and `release/v1.0/` - the frozen records of the previous identity cycles, kept byte for byte and never regenerated;\nL425: ## Documentation\nL426: \nL427: The 【112†online documentation†oraclizer.io】 covers installation, concepts, guides, the API reference, benchmarks, assurance and project policies for the current release line. The repository records are:\nL428:   * 【91†Architecture】\nL429:   * 【100†Formal verification】\nL430:   * 【116†Refinement evidence and reuse】\nL431:   * 【161†GKR base-session document snapshot (2026-08-29)】\nL432:   * 【162†Compiler document snapshot (2026-08-29)】\nL433:   * 【160†Frozen source identifiers】\nL434:   * 【163†Inner proof encoding】\nL435:   * 【155†Route manifest encoding】\nL436:   * 【164†Destination policy encoding】\nL437:   * 【165†Receipt-gated local transition】\nL438:   * 【114†Usage and API boundary】\nL439:   * 【166†Development measurements】\nL440:   * 【131†Serving components and reference server】\nL441:   * 【167†Troubleshooting】\nL442:   * 【105†Reproducing the release】\nL443:   * 【168†Release notes】\nL444:   * 【169†Release evidence】\nL445:   * 【156†Source reproduction】\nL446:   * 【92†Changelog】\nL447: ## Companion work\nL448: The 【170†Settlement EVM reference】 maintains destination receipt consumption and lifecycle authorities with its own toolchain, tests, and release cadence. Its 【171†relationship and compatibility map】 separates source compatibility from historical execution evidence. The V2 reference is not deployed, and historical V1 addresses must remain abandoned. This prover release contains proving and verification artifacts and does not distribute destination contracts or claim a production deployment.\nL449: ## Security, support, and contributing\nL450: \nL451: Report suspected vulnerabilities privately through 【106†SECURITY.md】, never in a public issue.\nL452: 【95†CONTRIBUTING.md】 describes the contributions that help most and exactly which checks a pull request must pass. 【107†SUPPORT.md】 describes what help is available and how triage works. 【101†GOVERNANCE.md】 describes how decisions are made. Community maintenance is best-effort, and there is no bug bounty. Commercial licensing for production use is arranged with Oraclizer Labs, Inc. through 【172†StateSync-GKR licensing†oraclizer.io】.\nL453: ## Acknowledgments\nL454: We thank Bernhard Müller (【173†@muellerberndt】) for his independent review of the verifier's challenge-generation assumptions and the implementation-facing interpretation of the extension-field soundness bound. His analysis clarified the distinction between the mechanized field/model results and the remaining multi-round Fiat–Shamir and input-binding arguments, and informed the corresponding claim corrections.\nL455: The project maintainer remains responsible for the implementation and the remaining security arguments.\nL456: 【96†Contributor and review history】 records the original authorship and the scope of this review.\nL457: ## License and citation\nL458: StateSync-GKR v1.1.1 and later versions are licensed under the 【102†Business Source License 1.1】. Research, development, evaluation, auditing, benchmarking and other non-production use are free, and production use solely to verify proofs is granted.\n"
